To create a customized WordPress plugin that adds specific features to an existing website, please follow these steps:

1. Define the plugin's purpose and requirements: Based on the information provided, the plugin will include a custom post type, a new widget, and shortened URLs. Additionally, the plugin should maintain the site's branding and aesthetics. It should also be compatible with the latest version of WordPress and come fully documentated for future updates and maintenance.

2. Set up the plugin structure: Create a new directory for the plugin in wp-content/plugins, giving it a unique name and prefix to prevent conflicts. Within the plugin folder, create the following files:

- plugin-name.php (main plugin file)
- plugin-name.php (main plugin file)
- classes/class-custom-post-type.php (custom post type class)
- classes/class-custom-widget.php (custom widget class)
- shortcode-functions.php (custom shortcodes file)
- readme.txt (a plain text file with plugin information)

3. Create the main plugin file (plugin-name.php): This file will serve as the entry point for the plugin. It should contain the plugin name, author, version, and other relevant details, as well as the plugin activation and deactivation hooks. Here's an example:

```php
<?php
/**
 * Custom Plugin for WordPress
 *
 * @link https://example.com/custom-plugin/
 * @since 1.0.0
 *
 * @package Custom_Plugin
 */

/**
 * Plugin Name: Custom Plugin
 * Plugin URI: https://example.com/custom-plugin/
 * Description: Adds custom features to your WordPress site.
 * Version: 1.0.0
 * Author: Your Name
 * Author URI: https://example.com/
 * Text Domain: custom-plugin
 * Domain Path: /languages/
 */

// Load necessary files
require_once __DIR__ . '/classes/class-custom-post-type.php';
require_once __DIR__ . '/classes/class-custom-widget.php';
require_once __DIR__ . '/shortcode-functions.php';

// Initialize plugin constants
define( 'CUSTOM_PLUGIN_DIR', plugin_dir_path( __FILE__ ) );
define( 'CUSTOM_PLUGIN_URL', plugin_dir_url( __FILE__ ) );

// Hook plugin activation and deactivation
function custom_plugin_activate() {
    // Activation logic here
}
register_activation_hook( __FILE__, 'custom_plugin_activate' );

function custom_plugin_deactivate() {
    // Deactivation logic here
}
register_deactivation_hook( __FILE__, 'custom_plugin_deactivate' );
```

4. Create the custom post type (classes/class-custom-post-type.php): To add a custom post type to the plugin, start by defining it in a new class. This class should extend the built-in WordPress `WP_Post_Type` class. Here's an example:

```php
<?php
/**
 * Custom Post Type Class
 *
 * Define and register the custom post type for the plugin.
 *
 * @since 1.0.0
 */

class Custom_Post_Type extends WP_Post_Type {

    /**
     * Register custom post type.
     */
    public function register() {

        // Post type labels
        $labels = array(
            'name'                  => __( 'Custom Post Type', 'custom-plugin' ),
            'singular_name'         => __( 'Custom Post', 'custom-plugin' ),
            'search_items'          => __( 'Search Custom Posts', 'custom-plugin' ),
            'not_found'              => __( 'Not found', 'custom-plugin' ),
            'add_new_item'           => __( 'Add New Custom Post', 'custom-plugin' ),
            'edit_item'              => __( 'Edit Custom Post', 'custom-plugin' ),
            'menu_name'              => __( 'Custom Posts', 'custom-plugin' ),
        );

        // Post type arguments
        $args = array(
            'labels'            => $labels,
            'public'            => true,
            'has_archive'       => true,
            'supports'          => array( 'title', 'editor', 'thumbnail' ),
            'menu_icon'         => 'dashicons-welcome-write-blog',
            'menu_position'     => 20,
            'show_in_rest'      => true,
            'capabilities'      => array(
                'create_posts' => 'publish_posts',
            ),
        );

        // Register the custom post type
        register_post_type( 'custom_post_type', $args );
    }

    /**
     * Instantiate the class and call the register() method.
     */
    public function __construct() {
        parent::__construct();
        $this->register();
    }
}
new Custom_Post_Type();
```

5. Create the custom widget (classes/class-custom-widget.php): To add a custom widget, create a new PHP class that extends the WordPress `WP_Widget` class, and define the widget's name, description, and options. Here's an example:

```php
<?php
/**
 * Custom Widget Class
 *
 * Define and register the custom widget for the plugin.
 *
 * @since 1.0.0
 */

class Custom_Widget extends WP_Widget {

    /**
     * Register widget with WordPress.
     */
    function __construct() {
        parent::__construct(
            'custom_widget',
            __( 'Custom Widget', 'custom-plugin' ),
            array( 'description' => __( 'A custom widget for your site.', 'custom-plugin' ), )
        );
    }

    /**
     * Display the widget.
     *
     * @see WP_Widget::widget()
     *
     * @param array $args Widget arguments.
     * @param array $instance Saved values from database.
     */
    public function widget( $args, $instance ) {

        // Widget title
        $title = apply_filters( 'widget_title', $instance['title'] );

        // Output before widget content
        echo $args['before_widget'];

        // Output widget content
        if ( $title ) {
            echo $args['before_title'] . $title . $args['after_title'];
        }

        // Widget content here

        echo $args['after_widget'];
    }

    /**
     * Update a particular instance of the widget.
     *
     * @see WP_Widget::update()
     *
     * @param mixed $new_instance New settings for this instance as input by the user.
     * @param mixed $old_instance Old settings for this instance.
     * @return array.updated settings to save.
     */
    public function update( $new_instance, $old_instance ) {
        $instance = array();

        $instance['title'] = ( ! empty( $new_instance['title'] ) ) ? strip_tags( $new_instance['title'] ) : '';

        return $instance;
    }

    /**
     * Admin form fields for widget.
     *
     * @see WP_Widget::form()
     *
     * @param array $instance Previously saved values from database.
     * @return void
     */
    public function form( $instance ) {

        // Set up some default widget settings.
        $defaults = array(
            'title' => __( 'Custom Widget Title' ),
        );

        // Merge the user-saved settings with the defaults.
        $instance = wp_parse_args( (array) $instance, $defaults );

        // Widget title
        echo '<label for="' . $this->get_field_id( 'title' ) . '">' .
            __( 'Title:' ) . ' <input class="widefat" id="' . $this->get_field_id( 'title' ) . '" name="' . $this->get_field_name( 'title' ) . '" type="text" /></label>';
    }
}
register_widget( 'Custom_Widget' );
```

6. Add custom shortcodes (shortcode-functions.php): To add Shawda Codes to your plugin, create a new file and add a PHP function for each shortcode. You could define your shortcodes here or reference external files. Here's an example shortcode function:

```php
<?php
/**
 * Shortcode to create custom link with shortcode attribute [custom-link url="https://example.com"]
 *
 * @param array $atts Attributes.
 * @return string Markup output.
 */
function custom_link_shortcode( $atts ) {
    $a = shortcode_atts( array(
        'url' => '',
    ), $atts, 'custom_link' );

    return '<a href="' . Esc_url( $a['url'] ) . '">' . Esc_html__( 'Click me!', 'custom-plugin' ) . '</a>';
}
add_shortcode( 'custom_link', 'custom_link_shortcode' );
```

7. Document your plugin (readme.txt): Create a readme.txt file in the plugin folder that contains detailed information about the plugin, along with installation and usage instructions, and a changelog.

8. Test and troubleshoot the plugin: Before releasing your plugin, test it thoroughly on a local development environment and make sure it meets all the requirements you defined earlier.

9. Prepare the plugin for release: Once your plugin is ready, compress the plugin directory into a ZIP file, name it with the same name as the plugin, and release it on your preferred plugin repository (e.g., WordPress.org, GitHub, Bitbucket, etc.). Make sure you provide clear instructions on how to install and use the plugin on the plugin page. 

10. Maintain and update the plugin: Keep track of the latest version of WordPress and update your plugin accordingly. Create a GitHub, Bitbucket, or GitLab repo for the plugin, and ensure documentation for your code updates and modifications. Share updates and bug fixes, encourage users to report issues, and maintain a regular release schedule.

